<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  One template for both services, rendered by deploy/install.sh.

  One rather than two because the daemon's plist and the control plane's differ
  only in a label, a program and a log name — everything a substitution can do.
  Two files identical but for three words is how two files stop being identical
  for a fourth reason nobody meant.

  A user agent (~/Library/LaunchAgents), not a system daemon. Both services keep
  all of their state under homedir() — databases and worktrees — so a
  system-wide unit would run them as a user whose home is not the one holding it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>@LABEL@</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@EXEC@</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>@REPO_ROOT@</string>

  <!--
    launchd starts a job with almost no environment: no profile is read, and PATH
    is a bare default that will not contain a Homebrew, Nix, nvm or corepack
    install of node. This value is resolved at install time from the operator's
    own PATH, which is why nothing here is written down.

    HOME is set explicitly for the same class of reason. It is normally present
    for a gui/ agent, but everything either service stores is under it.

    REEMOAT_ENV_FILE is here because it is a *runtime* override — the wrapper
    reads it on every start — and nothing propagates the installing shell's
    environment to a bootstrapped job. Without this line a packaged install run as
    `REEMOAT_ENV_FILE=/etc/reemoat/d.env install.sh daemon` configured one file
    and supervised another: either the wrapper found nothing at the default path
    and exited 2 into the KeepAlive/ThrottleInterval retry below, for ever, or —
    worse — it found a leftover default file and came up on the wrong token,
    the wrong REEMOAT_AUTH and the wrong database, while `wait_healthy` probed
    the port it read from the file it wrote and reported health: ok. Rendered
    unconditionally rather than only when the variable is set, so the unit names
    the file it reads instead of relying on the wrapper's fallback agreeing.
  -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>@PATH@</string>
    <key>HOME</key>
    <string>@HOME@</string>
    <key>REEMOAT_ENV_FILE</key>
    <string>@ENV_FILE@</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Restart whenever it stops, for any reason. This is the half that is missing
    today: nothing brings either service back after a reboot, a logout or a
    crash, and nobody finds out until they reach for a session that is not there.

    ThrottleInterval bounds the retry when a service is failing at startup rather
    than running — a wrong REEMOAT_TOKEN, a port in use, or a second daemon
    against a database the first one still holds. Without it a misconfigured
    service busy-loops.
  -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>10</integer>

  <!--
    Both streams to one file, because that is the order things actually happened
    in. Nothing in src/ prints; the startup banner and every warning come from
    scripts/, and splitting them across two files interleaves them wrongly.

    Deliberately no ProcessType: the default is Standard, while "Background"
    tells macOS this job may be throttled — which is a reasonable thing to say
    about an indexer and a bad thing to say about a process holding WebSocket
    tunnels for a fleet.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>@LOG_DIR@/@SERVICE@.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>@LOG_DIR@/@SERVICE@.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
